          correct: 1,
          explain:
            `As a bolide enters the atmosphere, dynamic (ram) pressure P_ram = ½ρ_atm v² acts on its leading face. When P_ram exceeds the bulk compressive strength of the impactor (typically 1–50 MPa for stony asteroids, up to ~200 MPa for iron meteorites), the body catastrophically fragments into a cloud of smaller pieces that rapidly ablate and deposit their energy over a short altitude range — the "pancake" model of airburst. The Tunguska 1908 event released ~10–15 Mt TNT equivalent at ~8–10 km altitude, flattening ~2,000 km² of Siberian forest with no macroscopic crater. The Chelyabinsk 2013 event (~0.5 Mt, altitude ~30 km) generated a powerful pressure wave that shattered windows across the Ural region and injured ~1,500 people. Both were caused by stony bodies estimated at ~60–80 m (Tunguska) and ~17–20 m (Chelyabinsk). Stony asteroids are prevalent in the metre-to-hundreds-of-metres size range and have modest mechanical strength, making airbursts the dominant outcome for this size class. Iron meteorites (strength ~200 MPa) are more likely to survive intact and form craters (e.g., Barringer). Option A is incorrect; Tunguska is now considered a stony asteroid, not a comet; composition determines strength but stony bodies are not guaranteed to airburst — the size-strength-velocity combination matters. Option C is incorrect; airburst and surface impact are mechanistically distinct based on where peak energy deposition occurs. Option D is incorrect; entry angle affects the atmospheric path length and therefore the altitude of peak pressure, but it is not the sole determinant of airburst vs. surface impact.`,
